    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "title",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "# Keyword Match Strategy"
      },
      {
        "id": "executive_summary",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "executive_source",
        "body": "## Executive Summary\n\n- **Use Exact as the account core and Phrase as a controlled expansion layer.** Exact produced 62.5% of seven-day clicks with a 5.68% CTR, versus 37.5% of clicks and a 4.35% CTR from Phrase. A practical interim allocation is roughly 65-75% of search spend to Exact and 25-35% to Phrase until conversion data becomes available.\n- **Phrase is the recent volume accelerator, not the final traffic filter.** It generated 276 of its 282 thirty-day impressions in the last seven days, but its directional Search impression share was only 59.8% and about 40.2% was lost to rank. Scale it only through device-qualified themes and search-term exclusions.\n- **Landing-page experience is the clearest verified quality constraint.** All 81 keywords with a current Quality Score were marked below average for landing-page experience. Separate detector, radar, and portable/handheld pages should come before broad bid increases.\n- **The recommendation is provisional because the account recorded zero conversions.** Traffic quality currently has to be judged from CTR, query intent, Quality Score and auction position rather than lead efficiency."
      },
      {
        "id": "metric_strip",
        "type": "metric-strip",
        "cardIds": [
          "impressions_card",
          "clicks_card",
          "ctr_card",
          "mix_card"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "definitions",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## How to read the comparison\n\nThe recent window is July 6-12. Because the 30-day report ends on the same date, subtracting the seven-day totals leaves a preceding 23-day comparison window. Search impression-share values are directional keyword-level estimates rather than additive account totals. The files do not contain search terms or a close-variant classification, so close-variant volume cannot be measured from these exports."
      },
      {
        "id": "match_finding",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "match_source",
        "body": "## Exact should remain the core, while Phrase supplies reach\n\nExact still produced 352 impressions and 20 clicks in the recent week, compared with 276 impressions and 12 clicks from Phrase. Exact also held a stronger auction position: approximately 82.4% Search impression share and 17.6% rank loss, versus 59.8% and 40.2% for Phrase. Phrase activated more efficiently—30 of 242 keywords received impressions, compared with 97 of 2,155 Exact keywords—so it is useful for discovery, but not as the sole traffic source."
      },
      {
        "id": "match_detail",
        "type": "table",
        "tableId": "match_table"
      },
      {
        "id": "growth_finding",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "headline_source",
        "body": "## Volume accelerated, but CTR weakened\n\nRecent daily impressions rose to 89.7 from 18.3 in the preceding 23 days, while daily clicks rose to 4.57 from 1.57. CTR nevertheless fell from 8.53% to 5.10%. The account is finding more auctions, but the incremental traffic is less selective, which supports a harvest model: discover through Phrase, then move proven commercial queries into Exact and exclude irrelevant searches."
      },
      {
        "id": "campaign_finding",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "campaign_source",
        "body": "## South America supports both layers; Middle East Phrase needs repair\n\nSouth America Phrase delivered 173 impressions and 10 clicks at a 5.78% CTR, while South America Detection Exact delivered 110 impressions and 11 clicks at a 10.0% CTR. Middle East Phrase produced 103 impressions but only two clicks, with a 1.94% CTR, about 38.3% Search impression share and 61.7% rank loss. Raising bids there before fixing language, query intent and page relevance would likely buy more weak traffic."
      },
      {
        "id": "campaign_chart_note",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "The campaign chart shows where the seven-day volume is concentrated. Use the companion table for exact CTR, auction position and Quality Score context."
      },
      {
        "id": "campaign_chart",
        "type": "chart",
        "chartId": "campaign_impressions_chart"
      },
      {
        "id": "campaign_detail",
        "type": "table",
        "tableId": "campaign_table"
      },
      {
        "id": "quality_finding",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "quality_source",
        "body": "## Landing-page relevance is a larger constraint than keyword coverage\n\nOf the 81 scored keyword rows, 63 were in the 1-3 Quality Score bucket. During the recent week, that bucket received 183 of the 246 impressions covered by Quality Score. More importantly, every scored keyword showed below-average landing-page experience. This is consistent with using one generic low-altitude monitoring page for detector, anti-drone system, radar, and handheld intent."
      },
      {
        "id": "quality_detail",
        "type": "table",
        "tableId": "quality_table"
      },
      {
        "id": "keyword_finding",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "keyword_source",
        "body": "## Proven Exact terms should define the core keyword set\n\nThe strongest thirty-day keyword was `[drone detector]` with 211 impressions and 25 clicks. Radar terms such as `[radar drone]` and other device-qualified terms also produced high CTR on small volume. Phrase winners such as `\"anti drone system\"`, `\"counter drone equipment\"`, and `\"uav detection radar\"` are useful discovery entries, but their underlying search terms should be reviewed before being treated as scalable demand."
      },
      {
        "id": "keyword_detail",
        "type": "table",
        "tableId": "keyword_table"
      },
      {
        "id": "recommendations",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Recommended next steps\n\n1. **Keep a two-layer structure.** Use Exact for proven device and procurement themes; keep Phrase at roughly 25-35% of search spend as a controlled discovery layer. The current seven-day spend split is already close to this range.\n2. **Prioritize South America.** Retain South America Detection Exact and South America Phrase. Do not materially expand Middle East Phrase until search terms, language and ad-to-page relevance improve.\n3. **Use device-qualified Phrase entries only.** Prefer `drone detector`, `drone detection system`, `anti drone system`, `counter drone equipment`, `uav detection radar`, and `handheld/portable drone detector`. Avoid generic `anti drone` or `drone detect` entries without a product noun.\n4. **Run a weekly harvest loop.** Promote a commercially relevant search term into Exact when it repeats or earns meaningful clicks; add immediate negatives for clearly irrelevant intent. Do not duplicate every close variant as a new keyword.\n5. **Build intent-specific landing pages before broad bid increases.** The first three should cover Drone Detector, Drone Radar, and Portable/Handheld RF Detector.\n6. **Fix conversion measurement.** With zero recorded conversions, the account cannot yet optimize toward qualified inquiries or prove which matching layer is more profitable."
      },
      {
        "id": "questions",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Further questions\n\n- Which exact search terms were classified as close variants, and which keyword/ad group received them?\n- Are WhatsApp, form submission, email and phone actions configured as primary conversions with duplicate prevention?\n- Are Middle East ads and landing pages localized for the actual countries and languages being targeted?\n- Does the search-term report show research, consumer, employment, DIY, software-only or military-news intent that should be excluded?"
      },
      {
        "id": "caveats",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Caveats and assumptions\n\nThe seven-day period is nested inside the 30-day period and Phrase traffic appears to have started or accelerated recently, so it should be observed for at least another one to two weeks. Quality Score is available for only 81 keywords and no Phrase keyword has a current score in these exports. Impression-share estimates are weighted directional summaries; `<10%` is represented as 5% and `>90%` as 95%. The exports do not include Search lost impression share due to budget, search terms, or close-variant classification. No conversion was recorded in either period."
      }
    ]
  },
